cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this work, please cite it as below."
title: "On the equation n'' = n and a problem of Erdos and Barbeau on products of prime-reciprocal sums"
abstract: >-
  A structural study of Erdos Problem #307: do finite sets of distinct primes P, Q exist with
  (sum 1/p)(sum 1/q) = 1? The problem is identified with Conjecture 4 of Ufnarovski and Ahlander
  (2003), which states it in the arithmetic-derivative literature, so #307 and the two-cycle
  question n'' = n are the same problem. A barrier is proved and machine-checked in Lean 4: any
  solution has |P u Q| >= 60 and min(prod P, prod Q) >= 2.09e56, for both members and without
  hypotheses. This appears to be the first valid unconditional bound of its kind, the only prior
  candidate resting on an invalid step. Several approaches are closed with proofs, and the
  constructive route is given an exact cost accounting. The problem itself remains open.
